gh is 100\u00b0F, on July 21, 1942.","t_chg":"Miami has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 121 complete years: Miami's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.30\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.22 to +0.37), about +3.8\u00b0F since 1896. Miami's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.31\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.26 to +0.37), about +4.0\u00b0F since 1896.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 11 of 12 months. Highs rose in all 12 months.","t_rain":"Miami has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Miami's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.85 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.24 to +1.46), about +10.8 inches since 1898. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 67.4 inches a year. The wettest year was 1959 (89.33 in); the driest, 1907 (33.15 in). Miami almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.0 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 17 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}